Muse , set for an Oct. 23 release on Republic Records &#8212; and written and recorded at Pharrell&#8217;s studio at Louis Vuitton&#8217;s headquarters, where he&#8217;s men&#8217;s creative director &#8212; had Legend looking beyond evergreen piano standards and into a &#8220;Black James Bond aesthetic, but with a modern backbeat at its foundation.&#8221;

&#8220;You can hear traces of me and traces of him, but it sounds like something new for the both of us. The combination of me and Pharrell, on paper, I think, is a good idea. And the execution is even better,&#8221; he says of Muse in a press statement released Tuesday (Sept. 8).

&#8220;I think my repertoire has always been very diverse, but the piano-driven ballads have gotten the most traction throughout my career. I wanted to shake up the formula and do something fresh and new,&#8221; Legend notes of the pair&#8217;s new project, which calls upon influences like Nina Simone, Nat King Cole and Marvin Gaye.

&#8220;Daylight,&#8221; the first single from Muse , arrives on streaming services today. Legend previewed the song with a performance from Nashville that was broadcast during CBS&#8217; American Tailgate the night before.

Another album track, “Pray for Ya,”&nbsp;soundtracked Louis Vuitton’s Fall-Winter 2026 Men’s show.

Pharrell produced the entire album. He&#8217;s also a co-writer, and he&#8217;s a featured artist on six tracks.

&#8220;When Pharrell called me, I thought, let&#8217;s do this,&#8221; says Legend, whose guest vocal on the Clipse track &#8220;The Birds Don&#8217;t Sing&#8221; inspired Pharrell &#8212; who&#8217;d produced Clipse&#8217;s 2025 set Let God Sort &#8216;Em Out &#8212; to team up with Legend on a full album. &#8220;A lot of people would have imagined we&#8217;d done something like this already, but we hadn&#8217;t. It felt past due.&#8221;

John Legend, &#8216;Muse&#8217;

Republic Records

Just as Legend guested on Clipse&#8217;s album, Clipse features on the Muse track &#8220;Bodies on the Floor.&#8221;

Muse &#8216;s title is in part inspired by the nine Muses in ancient Greek mythology. It&#8217;s also the name of the record&#8217;s opening track, which chronicles a &#8220;tumultuous relationship between a man and his muse &#8230; against the romantic backdrop of Paris and high art,&#8221; Tuesday&#8217;s press release says. The &#8220;muse&#8221; of that track is just a character the duo came up with, though Legend adds, “Our ancestors are our muses. My music has always been me channeling the ancestors, taking what they gave us and making something new.”

&#8220;I recorded some songs on this album that [Pharrell] fully wrote, which I almost never do,&#8221; says Legend. He&#8217;s shared his Muse partner thanked him for his trust and patience throughout their collaborative process and adds, &#8220;It&#8217;s not about winning the argument or ego. It&#8217;s about the music winning. Every battle should be won by the art.&#8221;

&#8220;There are plenty of artists who get to a point in their career where they don&#8217;t make anything new. We can just play the hits; we can stay in our comfort zone. But I believe it&#8217;s important to keep the mindset that I have to continue to prove myself. I wanted this album to have that sense of urgency — to feel like something that needed to be made and is fully worthy of my audience&#8217;s attention,&#8221; Legend says.

